INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Mandiant, Kaspersky, and WIRED reports on 3CX compromise", "source_url": "https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/3cx-software-supply-chain-compromise", "text": "Evidence batch: Mandiant (SRC-003) says the initial compromise vector of 3CX's network was malicious software downloaded from Trading Technologies' website: a tampered X_TRADER installer. It describes VEILEDSIGNAL and the X_TRADER loader chain, then says the attacker used compiled Fast Reverse Proxy to move laterally in 3CX, harvested credentials, compromised Windows and macOS build environments, and deployed TAXHAUL/COLDCAT on Windows and POOLRAT on macOS. The trojanized 3CX DesktopApp 18.12.416 and earlier ran SUDDENICON, obtained C2 URLs from encrypted GitHub icons, and downloaded ICONICSTEALER. Mandiant tracks the activity as UNC4736, a suspected North Korean nexus cluster; it assesses with moderate confidence that UNC4736 is related to financially motivated North Korean AppleJeus activity, while noting weaker overlap with suspected APT43 clusters. Kaspersky (SRC-004) reports Gopuram deployed to fewer than ten machines, especially cryptocurrency companies, and attributes the campaign to Lazarus with medium-to-high confidence based on coexistence with AppleJeus, infrastructure overlap, loader-shellcode similarities, and Lazarus-linked domains; it says Gopuram was likely the main/final payload. WIRED (SRC-008) reports Mandiant's patient-zero reconstruction: a 3CX employee PC was infected via the earlier Trading Technologies/X_Trader supply-chain compromise, enabling spread through 3CX to a software-development server and corruption of the 3CX installer. WIRED states Mandiant did not investigate the original Trading Technologies compromise, believes it occurred before Nov 2021 based on signature evidence, and says the follow-on 3CX compromise occurred in early 2023. WIRED (SRC-002) reports CrowdStrike and SentinelOne attributed the operation to North Korean hackers, and Kaspersky observed surgical targeting of fewer than ten cryptocurrency-related machines, but notes goals and actual theft remained uncertain."}
